{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 6, :comments ["<b>but only the bread that he ate</b> — this is euphemistic language, in accordance with the words of our Sages (Bereshit Rabbah 86:6). And Rabbi Abraham said (Ibn Ezra on Genesis 39:6) that everything that was his was in Joseph's hand except the bread that he ate, for he would not even touch it, because he was a Hebrew, for such was the law of the Egyptians toward the Hebrews, that they would not touch their food, for it was an abomination to the Egyptians. And this is possible; or the meaning of the verse may be that his master did not know of Joseph anything that he took from all that was his — only the bread alone that Joseph would eat — not for other pleasures as young men do, and not to amass for himself wealth and property, in the manner stated (I Samuel 29:3), \"and I have found no fault in him from the day he came until this day.\" And \"he did not know what was with me in the house\" (Genesis 39:8) is a different matter: that he would not look to know what was with him in the house, for \"he did not know with him anything\" is the language of privation, and \"what in the house\" will be its fulfillment." "<b>Now Joseph was well-built and handsome</b> — Scripture mentions this here to say that because of his beauty, his master's wife cast her eyes upon him. And Rashi wrote (Rashi on Genesis 39:6) that because he saw himself ruling, he began to eat and drink and curl his hair, etc."], :book :genesis, :voice :ramban, :lang :en, :chapter 39, :notes {}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-22T03:41:59.646763907Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}